{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794144967,"nonce":"KgnpkXWL8ZNv24n0pfbKA9nEfcw=","title":"Main-thread AnimationScheduler::tick caps dt at 32 ms, so timelines run slower than wall clock on slow frames","message":"`AnimationScheduler::tick` and `tick_frame_inner` cap each step at `MAX_TICK_DT` (32 ms) and smooth it with an EMA. When a frame takes longer than that, animations advance slower than the clock. A first-frame stall should pause an animation rather than fast-forward it, but a device that always renders slowly gets every animation in slow motion.\n\nMeasured with a looping timeline (0 to 360 over 1000 ms) in mobile/example, 2026-11-08:\n- Pixel 10 Pro, main-thread `tick()`, frames of 41 ms median: 276 deg/s, 77% of the declared speed (32 / 41).\n- iPhone 12 Pro Max, background-thread scheduler (`start_background`), frames of 50 ms median: 358 deg/s, wall clock.\n\nSame app, two phones, animations at different speeds; the iPhone looked about 1.3x faster. The Android runner ticks on the main thread; the iOS runner starts the background thread, which ticks at the target rate whatever the render rate. After the mobile poll change (fc977db, see the GPU issue) the Pixel presents about 60 fps and rarely hits the cap, so this shows up on slow devices and in debug builds.\n\nOptions: run the same scheduler mode on both mobile runners; raise the cap on mobile and keep the spring substepping that the cap protects (a cap near 100 ms still stops a 280 ms compile stall from settling a spring in one tick); or document that animations slow with the frame rate. CSS transitions and animations are not affected: they take wall-clock dt from `elapsed_ms()` in both runners.","files":null}]}